=== 3. Findings

F1 (blocking) - stderr above about 5 KB kills the runner on Windows.
  llm.py lines 127-137: the stderr reader thread keeps reading in 1 KB
  chunks, stops after 4,096 bytes (`if total_err > 4096: break`) and then
  closes the pipe (`proc.stderr.close()`). A runner that writes more to
  stderr after that gets a broken pipe. P1 shows the effect: 2-5 KiB of
  stderr gives `Ok 'reply'` (3 of 3 each); 8 KiB and 64 KiB give
  `ModelUnavailable 'local model runner failed with exit 120'` (3 of 3
  each). Exit 120 is CPython failing to flush stderr at exit. llama.cpp
  writes its model-loading log (one line per GGUF key, tensor counts,
  sampler settings) to stderr, which is usually far above 5 KB, so the
  default runner the W5 brief names would fail on every turn.
  On POSIX the runner would instead get SIGPIPE or EPIPE. Not tested here
  (no POSIX machine in this session); the outcome is the same class.
  Fix: read stderr to EOF and keep only the last 4 KB (for example
  `collections.deque(maxlen=4)` of 1 KB chunks, or a bytearray trimmed after
  each read). Memory stays bounded; the pipe is never closed early. Add a
  test: a runner that writes 64 KiB to stderr and then a reply must return
  the reply.

F2 (blocking) - W5 item 2 is untested.
  Item 2 is "create the prompt file with os.open(O_CREAT | O_EXCL, 0o600)
  and the prompts directory 0o700". llm.py lines 70 and 76 do that. K1
  reverts both (O_TRUNC, 0o644, plain mkdir) and the llm tests still pass:
  "Ran 11 tests ... OK / exit 0: NOT CAUGHT". So the item can regress
  silently. AGENTS.md review rule 3: a break the suite does not catch makes
  the verdict FAIL.
  Fix: (a) a POSIX-only test (skipUnless os.name == "posix") that stats the
  prompt file while the runner holds it (the runner can stat its own
  argv path) and asserts mode 0o600, and stats the prompts directory for
  0o700; (b) a portable test that pre-creates a file at the path the next
  call will use (patch uuid4 to a fixed value) and asserts complete() fails
  closed instead of overwriting it. (a) cannot run on this Windows host;
  say so in the report and let CI's Linux leg run it.

F3 (non-blocking) - environment scrubbing (item 5) is untested.
  K2 makes `_scrubbed_env` return `dict(os.environ)`: NOT CAUGHT. Add a
  test: set a canary variable in os.environ, run a runner that prints its
  own environment, assert the canary is absent and every printed key is in
  the documented keep list (llm.py line 265 and 267).

F4 (non-blocking) - a failed unlink is silent.
  llm.py lines 91-95: if prompt_file.unlink() raises OSError, the error is
  swallowed. P2 shows the case: a runner that leaves a child process holding
  the file open on Windows makes the unlink fail, complete() returns
  `Ok 'reply'`, and the prompt file with the token stays in the prompts
  directory ("token inside: True"). The reviewer removed it by hand.
  Fix: on OSError, retry once after the process has exited; if it still
  fails, raise ModelUnavailable (the reply is lost, but the owner is told),
  or at least log the filename (never the content) to stderr. Note in
  SCOPE.md that Windows file locks can defeat the delete.

F5 (non-blocking) - the test scanner covers system temp and cwd only.
  test_llm_privacy.py line 297: roots = [tempfile.gettempdir(), Path.cwd()].
  K5 writes the prompt to the home directory: NOT CAUGHT. The w5 harness
  has the same three roots. That matches the work order's wording, so this
  is a documented limit, not a defect: state it in the test docstring.

What holds:
  - Item 1 (stdin default): K3 is CAUGHT by
    test_config_defaults_use_stdin_to_true.
  - The leak tests catch a copy written to system temp: K4 CAUGHT by two
    tests, including the full Companion turn with a stored memory.
  - Item 6 (bounded output): P4 returns exactly 1,000 chars plus the
    truncation marker. freebuff's BREAK 2 shows the bound is load-bearing.
  - P3: UTF-8 with 2-, 3- and 4-byte characters round-trips (7,003 chars).
  - P5: a 1 MiB prompt reaches the runner whole on stdin (1,048,576 bytes),
    so the stdin writer thread does not deadlock against stdout.
  - companion.py lines 31-32: a ModelUnavailable message goes into the reply
    and the in-memory history only. F1's error text is not written to disk.
  - The count correction 48 to 47 was acknowledged by the author.

