=== 9. K1c on Linux: code-read plus CI, not a run
K1c is NOT CAUGHT here because test_prompt_file_and_dir_permissions_posix
is skipUnless(os.name == "posix") and Windows ignores POSIX modes. I could
not run the break on Linux (no WSL on this host: `wsl.exe -l -q` prints
"The Windows Subsystem for Linux is not installed."). What I can show:
- The test is not skipped in CI. Check run 38808871723 (commit 39b96af),
  stage 4, from `gh run view 38808871723 --log`:
    2026-10-15T21:19:33.5836145Z test_prompt_file_and_dir_permissions_posix (test_llm_privacy.LLMPrivacyTests.test_prompt_file_and_dir_permissions_posix)
    2026-10-15T21:19:33.6022611Z F2 (POSIX): Prompt file must have mode 0o600 and prompt directory mode 0o700 on POSIX. ... ok
    2026-10-15T21:19:34.2588922Z Ran 51 tests in 4.203s
    2026-10-15T21:19:34.2589105Z OK
  (51 tests, "OK" with no "skipped": every test ran on Linux.)
- The oracle is independent of the code under test: a separate runner
  process calls os.stat on the prompt file and its parent and prints
  oct(S_IMODE(...)) (test_llm_privacy.py lines 408-414); the test asserts
  "0o600" and "0o700" (lines 425-426). With K1c, the file is created with
  0o644; under the usual umask 022 the runner would print "0o644" and line
  425 would fail. A umask of 077 would hide the break. That is inferred
  from the code, not observed.

